What to notice:

unwrap_or_else(|_| die("not a number")) type-checks because the closure returns !, which coerces to u32.
let Ok(port) = …; and let Ok(v) = c.read(); have no else and no match. The compiler knows Err(!) cannot be constructed. With Result&lt;u16, String&gt; the same line is a compile error (refutable pattern).
Counter::Error = ! documents “cannot fail” in the type, and callers that are generic over Source still handle Self::Error normally.
Result&lt;u32, !&gt; is the same size as a bare u32: no tag is stored for a variant that cannot exist.
fn(Infallible) -&gt; ! accepting |x| x is the alias at work: the parameter is a !.

The same file on 1.99 stable stops at the first ! in type position:
