  |                      +++
The compiler’s help suggests pub String. In this case that is the wrong fix, because it would let anyone build an unvalidated Email. The privacy is the whole point.
The validation here is deliberately small. Real email validation belongs in a dedicated crate. The pattern stays the same whatever rule you plug in.

4. Getting around the orphan rule
You can’t implement a trait from another crate (Display) for a type from another crate (Vec&lt;String&gt;):
use std::fmt;

impl fmt::Display for Vec&lt;String&gt; {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter&lt;'_&gt;) -&gt; fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}", self.join(","))
    }
}

fn main() {}
$ cargo build -q --bin orphan_bug
error[E0117]: only traits defined in the current crate can be implemented for types defined outside of the crate
 --&gt; src/bin/orphan_bug.rs:3:1
  |
3 | impl fmt::Display for Vec&lt;String&gt; {
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^-----------
  |                       |
  |                       `Vec` is not defined in the current crate
  |
  = note: impl doesn't have any local type before any uncovered type parameters
  = note: for more information see https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/items/implementations.html#orphan-rules
  = note: define and implement a trait or new type instead
The last note gives the fix, “define and implement a trait or new type instead”:
use std::fmt;

// impl fmt::Display for Vec&lt;String&gt; { ... }  // E0117: only traits defined in
// the current crate can be implemented for types defined outside of the crate

struct Csv(Vec&lt;String&gt;);

impl fmt::Display for Csv {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter&lt;'_&gt;) -&gt; fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}", self.0.join(","))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let row = Csv(vec!["id".into(), "name".into(), "email".into()]);
    println!("{row}");
}
$ cargo run -q --bin orphan
id,name,email
The Book presents exactly this use. The orphan rules exist so that two crates can never provide conflicting impls of the same trait for the same type.

Annotating Lifetimes in Functions and Structs
When a function returns a reference that could come from multiple inputs, explicit lifetime annotations are required:
// The returned reference is guaranteed valid for the intersection of 'a
fn longest&lt;'a&gt;(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -&gt; &'a str {
    if x.len() &gt; y.len() { x } else { y }
}

struct Excerpt&lt;'a&gt; {
    part: &'a str,
}

fn main() {
    let string1 = String::from("desk configuration");
    let result;
    {
        let string2 = String::from("orders");
        result = longest(string1.as_str(), string2.as_str());
        println!("Longest string: {}", result);
    }
}

Lifetime Bounds on Traits
Constrain types with lifetime bounds, such as T: 'a (meaning T must outlive lifetime 'a):
use std::fmt::Display;

fn print_ref&lt;'a, T&gt;(item: &'a T)
where
    T: Display + 'a,
{
    println!("Item: {}", item);
}

Covariant: shared references shrink freely
// &'a T is covariant in 'a: a longer-lived reference can stand in for a shorter one.
fn pick&lt;'a&gt;(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -&gt; &'a str { if x.len() &gt;= y.len() { x } else { y } }

fn main() {
    let forever: &'static str = "static text";
    let owned = String::from("local");
    let r = pick(forever, &owned); // &'static str shrinks to the local lifetime
    println!("{r}");
}
$ rustc --edition 2024 cov.rs && ./cov
static text
pick needs a single 'a for both arguments. The compiler chooses 'a as the lifetime of owned and shrinks the &'static str to fit. That is safe because a shared reference can only be read, and reading something for less time than it lives is never a problem.

Invariant: &mut T can’t shrink its T
Now make the shrinking happen behind a &mut:
// &'a mut T is invariant in T: this is what keeps the next line from compiling.
fn overwrite&lt;T&gt;(slot: &mut T, value: T) { *slot = value; }

fn main() {
    let mut s: &'static str = "static text";
    {
        let local = String::from("short-lived");
        overwrite(&mut s, &local);
    }
    println!("{s}");
}
$ rustc --edition 2024 inv_mut.rs && ./inv_mut
