Contravariance is rare in everyday code. In practice it comes from fn(T) fields and from PhantomData&lt;fn(T)&gt; markers.

The common case: &'a mut self on a type with 'a
Here is the variance bug most Rust programmers hit, usually in a parser, interner or arena:
// The "&'a mut Thing&lt;'a&gt;" trap: tying the borrow to the invariant parameter.
struct Interner&lt;'a&gt; { words: Vec&lt;&'a str&gt; }

impl&lt;'a&gt; Interner&lt;'a&gt; {
    fn add(&'a mut self, w: &'a str) { self.words.push(w); }
}

fn main() {
    let text = String::from("alpha beta");
    let mut i = Interner { words: Vec::new() };
    for w in text.split(' ') {
        i.add(w);
    }
    println!("{}", i.words.len());
}
$ rustc --edition 2024 vec_trap.rs && ./vec_trap
error[E0499]: cannot borrow `i` as mutable more than once at a time
  --&gt; vec_trap.rs:12:9
   |
12 |         i.add(w);
   |         ^
   |         |
   |         `i` was mutably borrowed here in the previous iteration of the loop
   |         first borrow used here, in later iteration of loop

error[E0502]: cannot borrow `i.words` as immutable because it is also borrowed as mutable
  --&gt; vec_trap.rs:14:20
   |
12 |         i.add(w);
   |         - mutable borrow occurs here
13 |     }
14 |     println!("{}", i.words.len());
   |                    ^^^^^^^
   |                    |
   |                    immutable borrow occurs here
   |                    mutable borrow later used here

error: aborting due to 2 previous errors

Some errors have detailed explanations: E0499, E0502.
For more information about an error, try `rustc --explain E0499`.
The signature fn add(&'a mut self, w: &'a str) says: “borrow self mutably for 'a, the same lifetime the struct holds its data for.” Interner&lt;'a&gt; contains Vec&lt;&'a str&gt;, and &mut Interner&lt;'a&gt; is invariant in Interner&lt;'a&gt;. The compiler can’t shrink 'a to make the borrow shorter, so the first call to add borrows i mutably for all of 'a, which is every remaining use of i. The second loop iteration can’t borrow it again, and neither can the println!.
The fix is to stop tying the borrow of self to the data lifetime. The &mut self borrow gets its own short, elided lifetime:
struct Interner&lt;'a&gt; { words: Vec&lt;&'a str&gt; }

impl&lt;'a&gt; Interner&lt;'a&gt; {
    fn add(&mut self, w: &'a str) { self.words.push(w); }
}

fn main() {
    let text = String::from("alpha beta");
    let mut i = Interner { words: Vec::new() };
    for w in text.split(' ') {
        i.add(w);
    }
    println!("{} words: {:?}", i.words.len(), i.words);
}
$ rustc --edition 2024 vec_fixed.rs && ./vec_fixed
2 words: ["alpha", "beta"]
As a rule: a &'a mut Thing&lt;'a&gt; signature is almost always a mistake. The same goes for &'a mut Vec&lt;&'a T&gt; in function signatures. Give the mutable borrow its own lifetime, which in most cases means leaving it elided.

Box for Heap Allocation
Box&lt;T&gt; allocates a value on the heap and provides a stack pointer with ownership semantics.
fn main() {
    let b = Box::new(42);
    println!("Box points to heap value: {}", *b);

    // Recursive data types require Box to have a known size:
    #[derive(Debug)]
    enum List {
        Cons(i32, Box&lt;List&gt;),
        Nil,
    }

    let list = List::Cons(1, Box::new(List::Cons(2, Box::new(List::Nil))));
    println!("Recursive list: {:?}", list);
}

Reference Counting with Rc and Arc
When multiple parts of a program need shared read ownership of heap memory: - Rc&lt;T&gt;: Single-threaded reference counting. - Arc&lt;T&gt;: Atomic reference counting across multiple threads.
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let data = Arc::new(vec![1, 2, 3]);

    let data_clone = Arc::clone(&data);
    let handle = thread::spawn(move || {
        println!("Thread sees: {:?}", data_clone);
    });

    handle.join().unwrap();
    println!("Main sees: {:?}", data);
}

Interior Mutability with RefCell
RefCell&lt;T&gt; enforces Rust’s borrowing rules dynamically at runtime rather than at compile time:
use std::cell::RefCell;

fn main() {
    let val = RefCell::new(10);
    {
        let mut mut_borrow = val.borrow_mut();
        *mut_borrow += 5;
    }
    println!("Mutated value: {}", *val.borrow());
}

Cell and Mutex for Shared State

Cell&lt;T&gt;: Interior mutability for Copy types via value copying.
Mutex&lt;T&gt;: Thread-safe interior mutability via mutual exclusion locks.

use std::sync::Mutex;

fn main() {
    let m = Mutex::new(0);
    {
        let mut guard = m.lock().unwrap();
        *guard += 1;
    }
    println!("Mutex protected value: {:?}", m.lock().unwrap());
}

Weak References and Breaking Cycles
Cycles of Rc or Arc pointers can cause memory leaks. Use Weak&lt;T&gt; (downgraded non-owning reference) to break reference cycles:
use std::rc::{Rc, Weak};

struct Node {
    parent: Option&lt;Weak&lt;Node&gt;&gt;,
}

Worked examples

Case 1: LazyLock for a process-wide regex
use std::sync::LazyLock;
use regex::Regex;

static EMAIL: LazyLock&lt;Regex&gt; = LazyLock::new(|| {
    Regex::new(r"(?i)^[a-z0-9._%+-]+@[a-z0-9.-]+\.[a-z]{2,}$").expect("static regex")
});

fn looks_like_email(s: &str) -&gt; bool {
    EMAIL.is_match(s)
}

fn main() {
    assert!(looks_like_email("dev@example.com"));
    assert!(!looks_like_email("not-an-email"));
    println!("ok");
}
First call to looks_like_email compiles the pattern once. Later calls reuse the same Regex. The expect is acceptable only for patterns that are compile-time constants you control — never for user-supplied patterns inside a LazyLock init.
Add regex in Cargo.toml for this sample (regex = "1"). For a std-only sketch, replace the body with a pure function that builds a String or HashMap.


Case 2: OnceLock when init needs an argument
use std::sync::OnceLock;

struct AppConfig {
    api_root: String,
}

fn config() -&gt; &'static AppConfig {
    static CFG: OnceLock&lt;AppConfig&gt; = OnceLock::new();
    CFG.get_or_init(|| AppConfig {
        api_root: std::env::var("API_ROOT").unwrap_or_else(|_| "http://127.0.0.1:8080".into()),
    })
}

fn main() {
    println!("api_root={}", config().api_root);
    // Second call returns the same address
    assert!(std::ptr::eq(config(), config()));
}
get_or_init runs the closure only if empty. set / get let you initialize from main after CLI parsing:
use std::sync::OnceLock;

static CFG: OnceLock&lt;String&gt; = OnceLock::new();

fn main() {
    let root = std::env::args().nth(1).unwrap_or_else(|| "/srv/app".into());
    CFG.set(root).expect("config set once");
    println!("{}", CFG.get().expect("configured"));
}
set returns Err(value) if the cell was already filled — use that to catch double-init bugs in tests.


Case 3: Fallible init without poisoning LazyLock
use std::sync::OnceLock;

static DB_URL: OnceLock&lt;String&gt; = OnceLock::new();

fn init_db_url() -&gt; Result&lt;(), String&gt; {
    let url = std::env::var("DATABASE_URL").map_err(|_| "DATABASE_URL missing".to_string())?;
    if !url.starts_with("postgres://") {
        return Err("DATABASE_URL must start with postgres://".into());
    }
    DB_URL.set(url).map_err(|_| "DB_URL already set".to_string())?;
    Ok(())
}

fn db_url() -&gt; &'static str {
    DB_URL.get().expect("call init_db_url() before use")
}

fn main() {
    if let Err(e) = init_db_url() {
        eprintln!("startup failed: {e}");
        std::process::exit(1);
    }
    println!("using {}", db_url());
}
Validate and then set. Do not hide I/O errors inside LazyLock::new(|| …) if you need a clean startup failure path.


Case 4: Single-threaded LazyCell
use std::cell::LazyCell;

fn main() {
    let cache = LazyCell::new(|| {
        println!("computing");
        21 * 2
    });
    assert_eq!(*cache, 42); // prints "computing"
    assert_eq!(*cache, 42); // silent
}
Use LazyCell / OnceCell inside one thread (or inside a type that is already !Sync). Crossing threads wants LazyLock / OnceLock.

Worked examples

Case 1: write then assume_init
use std::mem::MaybeUninit;

fn main() {
    let mut slot = MaybeUninit::&lt;Vec&lt;u8&gt;&gt;::uninit();
    slot.write(vec![1, 2, 3]);
    let v = unsafe { slot.assume_init() };
    assert_eq!(v, vec![1, 2, 3]);
    println!("len={}", v.len());
}
write returns &mut T to the now-initialized contents. Calling write twice skips dropping the first value — a leak if T owns heap memory. Prefer one write, or drop explicitly between overwrites when you must replace.


Case 2: Out-pointer style
use std::mem::MaybeUninit;

unsafe fn fill_vec(out: *mut Vec&lt;i32&gt;) {
    // write does not drop old contents — correct for uninit destination
    unsafe { out.write(vec![10, 20, 30]); }
}

fn main() {
    let mut slot = MaybeUninit::uninit();
    unsafe { fill_vec(slot.as_mut_ptr()); }
    let v = unsafe { slot.assume_init() };
    assert_eq!(v, vec![10, 20, 30]);
}
The caller owns allocation strategy; the callee only initializes. After assume_init, Drop for Vec runs normally at end of scope.


Case 3: Array element-by-element
use std::mem::{self, MaybeUninit};

fn build_boxes() -&gt; [Box&lt;u32&gt;; 4] {
    let mut data: [MaybeUninit&lt;Box&lt;u32&gt;&gt;; 4] = [const { MaybeUninit::uninit() }; 4];

    for (i, slot) in data.iter_mut().enumerate() {
        slot.write(Box::new(i as u32));
    }

    // SAFETY: every element was written
    unsafe { mem::transmute::&lt;_, [Box&lt;u32&gt;; 4]&gt;(data) }
}

fn main() {
    let arr = build_boxes();
    assert_eq!(*arr[3], 3);
    println!("{:?}", arr.iter().map(|b| **b).collect::&lt;Vec&lt;_&gt;&gt;());
}
Panic mid-loop leaks already-written Boxes (no destructor on remaining MaybeUninit), but does not invent UB from dropping uninitialized boxes. For partial init, track a length and call assume_init_drop on the live prefix in a cleanup path.


Case 4: Field-by-field struct init
use std::mem::MaybeUninit;

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
struct Person {
    name: String,
    age: u32,
}

fn main() {
    let person = {
        let mut uninit = MaybeUninit::&lt;Person&gt;::uninit();
        let ptr = uninit.as_mut_ptr();

        // SAFETY: write through raw field pointers — do NOT create &mut to uninit fields
        unsafe {
            (&raw mut (*ptr).name).write(String::from("Ada"));
            (&raw mut (*ptr).age).write(36);
            uninit.assume_init()
        }
    };

    assert_eq!(
        person,
        Person {
            name: String::from("Ada"),
            age: 36
        }
    );
    println!("{person:?}");
}
Using assume_init_mut() to “assign fields” before the struct is fully initialized is UB — that creates references to uninitialized memory. Prefer &raw mut / ptr::write as above. Padding bytes need not be initialized.


Case 5: zeroed pitfalls
use std::mem::MaybeUninit;

fn main() {
    // OK: (u8, bool) can be all-zero
    let pair = unsafe { MaybeUninit::&lt;(u8, bool)&gt;::zeroed().assume_init() };
    assert_eq!(pair, (0, false));

    // NOT OK: &T must be non-null — zeroed reference is instantaneous UB
    // let bad: &i32 = unsafe { MaybeUninit::zeroed().assume_init() };

    // NOT OK: many enums reject discriminant 0
    // enum NonZero { A = 1, B = 2 }
    // let bad = unsafe { MaybeUninit::&lt;NonZero&gt;::zeroed().assume_init() };

    println!("pair={pair:?}");
}
Rule of thumb: zeroed().assume_init() only for types where every bit pattern of all-zeros is a valid value (raw integers, bool, structs of such). Never for references, NonNull, most enums, or “looks like a Vec with ptr=0”.

