Implementing the trait is a safety contract
unsafe impl Allocator is a promise the compiler cannot check. The ways to break it are the classic C allocator bugs: handing out overlapping blocks (a bump cursor that forgets alignment padding), returning memory that dies before the container does (an allocator that owns its buffer by value and gets moved), or panicking inside allocate. The stabilized contract explicitly forbids unwinding out of any Allocator method, from its clones, or from its drop. Keep the unsafe impl small, put the bookkeeping in plain safe code, and test it under Miri (cargo +nightly miri test), which catches the use-after-free, out-of-bounds, and aliasing violations that a broken allocator causes in the containers using it.

The Iterator Trait and Protocol
In Rust, iterators are lazy sequences that produce values on demand via the Iterator trait:
fn main() {
    let v = vec![1, 2, 3];
    let mut iter = v.iter();

    assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);
}

Creating Custom Iterators
struct Counter {
    count: usize,
}

impl Counter {
    fn new() -&gt; Self { Self { count: 0 } }
}

impl Iterator for Counter {
    type Item = usize;
    fn next(&mut self) -&gt; Option&lt;Self::Item&gt; {
        if self.count &lt; 3 {
            self.count += 1;
            Some(self.count)
        } else {
            None
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let counter = Counter::new();
    let items: Vec&lt;usize&gt; = counter.collect();
    println!("Custom iterator output: {:?}", items);
}

Closures and Capture Semantics
Closures are anonymous functions that can capture environment variables: - Fn: Borrows values from the environment immutably. - FnMut: Borrows values mutably. - FnOnce: Takes ownership and consumes captured values.
