Case 1: Same throughput, very different latency
The program runs 64 CPU-heavy “requests” twice: once calling Rayon inline in the handler and once offloaded. Meanwhile a task ticks every 5 ms, standing in for everything else the service must keep doing, and reports the worst lateness of any tick.
// src/main.rs — CPU work inside Tokio: blocking the workers vs. a separate Rayon lane
use rayon::prelude::*;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering};
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
use tokio::sync::{oneshot, Semaphore};

/// Stand-in for a CPU-heavy request: pure computation, parallelised with Rayon.
fn evaluate(batch: &[u64]) -&gt; u64 {
    batch
        .par_iter()
        .map(|&x| (0..20_000u64).fold(x, |a, i| a.wrapping_mul(6364136223846793005).wrapping_add(i)))
        .sum()
}

/// Wrong: call Rayon directly on a Tokio worker thread.
async fn handle_inline(batch: Arc&lt;Vec&lt;u64&gt;&gt;) -&gt; u64 {
    evaluate(&batch)
}

/// Right: bound in-flight batches, hand the work to Rayon, await a oneshot.
async fn handle_offloaded(batch: Arc&lt;Vec&lt;u64&gt;&gt;, permits: Arc&lt;Semaphore&gt;) -&gt; u64 {
    let _permit = permits.acquire_owned().await.expect("semaphore closed");
    let (tx, rx) = oneshot::channel();
    rayon::spawn(move || {
        let _ = tx.send(evaluate(&batch));
    });
    rx.await.expect("rayon job dropped its sender")
}

/// A 5 ms ticker standing in for health checks and socket polling.
/// Returns the worst lateness of any tick until `stop` is set.
async fn ticker(stop: Arc&lt;AtomicBool&gt;) -&gt; Duration {
    let mut worst = Duration::ZERO;
    let mut next = tokio::time::Instant::now();
    while !stop.load(Ordering::Relaxed) {
        next += Duration::from_millis(5);
        tokio::time::sleep_until(next).await;
        worst = worst.max(tokio::time::Instant::now() - next);
    }
    worst
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let batch = Arc::new((0..2_000u64).collect::&lt;Vec&lt;_&gt;&gt;());
    let requests = 64;
    println!(
        "available_parallelism: {}, tokio workers: {}, rayon threads: {}",
        std::thread::available_parallelism().unwrap(),
        tokio::runtime::Handle::current().metrics().num_workers(),
        rayon::current_num_threads()
    );

    for mode in ["inline", "offloaded"] {
        let stop = Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(false));
        let tick = tokio::spawn(ticker(stop.clone()));
        let permits = Arc::new(Semaphore::new(2));
        let start = Instant::now();

        let mut set = tokio::task::JoinSet::new();
        for _ in 0..requests {
            let (b, p) = (batch.clone(), permits.clone());
            set.spawn(async move {
                match mode {
                    "inline" =&gt; handle_inline(b).await,
                    _ =&gt; handle_offloaded(b, p).await,
                }
            });
        }
        while set.join_next().await.is_some() {}

        let elapsed = start.elapsed();
        stop.store(true, Ordering::Relaxed);
        let lag = tick.await.unwrap();
        println!(
            "{mode:&gt;9}: {requests} requests in {:&gt;4} ms, worst tick lag {:&gt;3} ms",
            elapsed.as_millis(),
            lag.as_millis()
        );
    }
}
cargo run --release
available_parallelism: 8, tokio workers: 8, rayon threads: 8
   inline: 64 requests in   62 ms, worst tick lag  52 ms
offloaded: 64 requests in   61 ms, worst tick lag   2 ms
Two more runs gave 55/2 ms and 47/2 ms. Total work time is the same either way, because the same CPUs do the same arithmetic. What changes is whether the runtime stays responsive. Inline, the 5 ms ticker was blocked for most of the batch: in a real service, that is a health check or a socket read waiting 50 ms. Offloaded, it never slipped more than 2 ms.


Case 2: Under a CPU limit
Restrict the process to two CPUs, as a container limit or taskset would:
taskset -c 0-1 ./target/release/lanes
available_parallelism: 2, tokio workers: 2, rayon threads: 2
   inline: 64 requests in  210 ms, worst tick lag 204 ms
offloaded: 64 requests in  212 ms, worst tick lag   3 ms
With two workers, inline Rayon froze the runtime for the entire batch. Both pools shrank to two threads because available_parallelism() follows the affinity mask (and on Linux the cgroup CPU quota, too). That is usually what you want, but it is computed from the limit, not the request. In Kubernetes, a pod with requests: 2, limits: 8 will start eight threads per pool and get throttled in bursts. Set both pools explicitly:
// pools.rs — explicit thread budget for both lanes
fn main() {
    let cpus = std::env::var("CPU_BUDGET").ok().and_then(|v| v.parse().ok()).unwrap_or(2);

    rayon::ThreadPoolBuilder::new()
        .num_threads(cpus)                       // compute lane: all of the budget
        .thread_name(|i| format!("rayon-{i}"))
        .build_global()
        .expect("rayon pool already initialised");

    let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_multi_thread()
        .worker_threads((cpus / 2).max(1))       // I/O lane: mostly parked in .await
        .enable_all()
        .build()
        .unwrap();
    rt.block_on(async { /* serve */ });
}
Mild oversubscription (Rayon gets the whole budget, Tokio half) is fine because Tokio’s workers spend most of their time parked.


Case 3: Bound the work in flight
handle_offloaded takes a permit from a Semaphore::new(2) before it spawns. Rayon itself never refuses work, so the semaphore is the only thing that turns a burst into async waiting (cheap, cancellable, visible as a queue length you can measure and time out) instead of a growing pile of CPU jobs. Add a timeout around the whole handler to shed load:
match tokio::time::timeout(Duration::from_millis(250), handle_offloaded(b, p)).await {
    Ok(v) =&gt; Ok(v),
    Err(_) =&gt; Err("busy, try again"), // the permit was never taken or is released on drop
}
Small batches should skip Rayon entirely. Below a few hundred items, the cost of splitting and joining is larger than the work. Measure the threshold for your own data.

The trap
A panic in a rayon::spawn job is not your request’s error. With tokio::spawn, a panic becomes a JoinError for that one task. With rayon::spawn, the default is that Rayon aborts the process. Run this without a panic handler and the output ends with Rayon: detected unexpected panic; aborting. In a server, that kills every in-flight request.
// examples/panic.rs — what a panic inside rayon::spawn looks like from the async side
use tokio::sync::oneshot;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // Without a panic handler, a panic in rayon::spawn aborts the whole process.
    // Production pools install one; this is the usual "log and carry on" handler.
    rayon::ThreadPoolBuilder::new()
        .panic_handler(|_| eprintln!("rayon: job panicked"))
        .build_global()
        .unwrap();

    let (tx, rx) = oneshot::channel::&lt;u64&gt;();
    rayon::spawn(move || {
        let batch: Vec&lt;u64&gt; = Vec::new();
        let first = batch[0]; // bad input: index out of bounds
        let _ = tx.send(first);
    });

    match rx.await {
        Ok(v) =&gt; println!("result: {v}"),
        Err(e) =&gt; println!("request failed: {e}"),
    }
}
cargo run --release --example panic
thread '&lt;unnamed&gt;' (379384) panicked at examples/panic.rs:16:26:
index out of bounds: the len is 0 but the index is 0
note: run with `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` environment variable to display a backtrace
rayon: job panicked
request failed: channel closed
With a panic_handler installed, the process survives. The sender is dropped while the job unwinds, so the handler sees RecvError (“channel closed”) and can return a 500 for that one request. The handler must not use .expect() on the receiver, as Case 1’s demo code does, because that just moves the panic into the Tokio task. The alternative is to catch the panic inside the job (std::panic::catch_unwind) and send a Result over the channel.

Worked examples
Tested with rustc 1.99.0 (Edition 2024) on x86_64 Linux. No crates beyond std.

Case 1: a length-prefixed framer with no sockets
Wire format: two big-endian length bytes, then that many payload bytes. The machine queues outbound frames, reassembles inbound chunks, and rejects declared lengths above a cap.
// src/main.rs — sans-IO length-prefixed framer
use std::collections::VecDeque;
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
struct Transmit {
    bytes: Vec&lt;u8&gt;,
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
enum Event {
    Message(Vec&lt;u8&gt;),
    ProtocolError(&'static str),
    TimedOut,
}

struct Framer {
    rx_buf: Vec&lt;u8&gt;,
    tx: VecDeque&lt;Transmit&gt;,
    events: VecDeque&lt;Event&gt;,
    max_frame: usize,
    incomplete_since: Option&lt;Instant&gt;,
    stall_for: Duration,
}

impl Framer {
    fn new(max_frame: usize) -&gt; Self {
        Self {
            rx_buf: Vec::new(),
            tx: VecDeque::new(),
            events: VecDeque::new(),
            max_frame,
            incomplete_since: None,
            stall_for: Duration::from_secs(5),
        }
    }

    /// App intent: enqueue a framed transmit. No I/O.
    fn send(&mut self, payload: &[u8]) -&gt; Result&lt;(), &'static str&gt; {
        if payload.len() &gt; self.max_frame {
            return Err("frame too large");
        }
        let len = u16::try_from(payload.len()).map_err(|_| "frame too large")?;
        let mut bytes = Vec::with_capacity(2 + payload.len());
        bytes.extend_from_slice(&len.to_be_bytes());
        bytes.extend_from_slice(payload);
        self.tx.push_back(Transmit { bytes });
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Driver feeds bytes read from somewhere (socket, test, file).
    fn handle_input(&mut self, chunk: &[u8], now: Instant) {
        if !chunk.is_empty() {
            self.rx_buf.extend_from_slice(chunk);
            self.incomplete_since = Some(now);
        }
        self.drain_frames();
        if self.rx_buf.is_empty() {
            self.incomplete_since = None;
        }
    }

    fn drain_frames(&mut self) {
        loop {
            if self.rx_buf.len() &lt; 2 {
                return;
            }
            let len = u16::from_be_bytes([self.rx_buf[0], self.rx_buf[1]]) as usize;
            if len &gt; self.max_frame {
                self.events
                    .push_back(Event::ProtocolError("declared length exceeds max_frame"));
                self.rx_buf.clear();
                return;
            }
            if self.rx_buf.len() &lt; 2 + len {
                return;
            }
            let payload = self.rx_buf[2..2 + len].to_vec();
            self.rx_buf.drain(..2 + len);
            self.events.push_back(Event::Message(payload));
        }
    }

    fn poll_timeout(&self) -&gt; Option&lt;Instant&gt; {
        self.incomplete_since.map(|t| t + self.stall_for)
    }

    fn handle_timeout(&mut self, now: Instant) {
        if let Some(start) = self.incomplete_since {
            if now &gt;= start + self.stall_for && !self.rx_buf.is_empty() {
                self.events.push_back(Event::TimedOut);
                self.rx_buf.clear();
                self.incomplete_since = None;
            }
        }
    }

    fn poll_transmit(&mut self) -&gt; Option&lt;Transmit&gt; {
        self.tx.pop_front()
    }

    fn poll_event(&mut self) -&gt; Option&lt;Event&gt; {
        self.events.pop_front()
    }
}

fn main() {
    let t0 = Instant::now();

    let mut a = Framer::new(64);
    a.send(b"ping").unwrap();
    a.send(b"pong-extra").unwrap();
    let mut wire = Vec::new();
    while let Some(t) = a.poll_transmit() {
        wire.extend_from_slice(&t.bytes);
    }
    println!("wire bytes: {} {:?}", wire.len(), wire);

    // Reassemble one byte at a time — no socket required.
    let mut b = Framer::new(64);
    for byte in &wire {
        b.handle_input(&[*byte], t0);
    }
    while let Some(ev) = b.poll_event() {
        match &ev {
            Event::Message(p) =&gt; println!("event: Message({:?})", String::from_utf8_lossy(p)),
            other =&gt; println!("event: {other:?}"),
        }
    }

    let mut c = Framer::new(8);
    c.handle_input(&[0x00, 0x20], t0); // claims 32 bytes; max is 8
    println!("oversize: {:?}", c.poll_event());

    // Time travel: incomplete header, then jump 5s without sleeping.
    let mut d = Framer::new(64);
    d.handle_input(&[0x00, 0x04], t0);
    let deadline = d.poll_timeout().expect("waiting for rest of frame");
    println!("poll_timeout deadline offset: {:?}", deadline - t0);
    d.handle_timeout(t0 + Duration::from_secs(5));
    println!("after time travel: {:?}", d.poll_event());
}
cargo run
wire bytes: 18 [0, 4, 112, 105, 110, 103, 0, 10, 112, 111, 110, 103, 45, 101, 120, 116, 114, 97]
event: Message("ping")
event: Message("pong-extra")
oversize: Some(ProtocolError("declared length exceeds max_frame"))
poll_timeout deadline offset: 5s
after time travel: Some(TimedOut)
Eighteen wire bytes are two frames (4 + ping, 10 + pong-extra). Feeding them one byte at a time still yields two messages. Declaring length 32 against max_frame = 8 is a protocol error, not a panic. The stall path fires because the driver passed t0 + 5s — the test never slept.


Case 2: the driver loop (sketch)
A blocking UDP driver is a thin shell. Replace recv_from with Tokio’s recv and the protocol code stays identical.
// driver_sketch.rs — not a full program; shows the poll/handle rhythm
fn drive(socket: &std::net::UdpSocket, proto: &mut Framer) -&gt; std::io::Result&lt;()&gt; {
    let mut buf = [0u8; 1500];
    loop {
        while let Some(t) = proto.poll_transmit() {
            socket.send(&t.bytes)?;
        }
        while let Some(ev) = proto.poll_event() {
            eprintln!("app event: {ev:?}");
        }
        socket.set_read_timeout(
            proto
                .poll_timeout()
                .map(|deadline| deadline.saturating_duration_since(std::time::Instant::now())),
        )?;
        match socket.recv(&mut buf) {
            Ok(n) =&gt; proto.handle_input(&buf[..n], std::time::Instant::now()),
            Err(e) if e.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::WouldBlock
                || e.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::TimedOut =&gt;
            {
                proto.handle_timeout(std::time::Instant::now());
            }
            Err(e) =&gt; return Err(e),
        }
    }
}
The protocol never imports std::net. Swap the socket for a VecDeque&lt;Vec&lt;u8&gt;&gt; in tests and you have a deterministic network.


Case 3: why this pairs with Rust
Async Rust wants 'static tasks once you spawn. Shared mutable protocol state then tends toward Arc&lt;Mutex&lt;_&gt;&gt; or channels — both fight the borrow checker you wanted. A sans-IO machine stays on the stack (or in one task) and mutates with &mut self. Firezone’s write-up puts it plainly: no tasks inside the protocol means no mutexes and no cross-task copies for the hot path. You still use Tokio for the driver; you stop using Tokio as the protocol’s control plane.

