Worked examples

Case 1: Minimal Drop smuggling (reproduced here)
Verified on this box: rustc 1.99.0 (b940084d7 2026-09-28). The program compiles; after the inner scope, me_out prints garbage (use-after-free of the String buffer).
// uaf_covariant_field.rs — safe Rust; reproduces rust#164250 shape (lcnr / theemathas)
// rustc 1.99.0: compiles and runs; output is nondeterministic garbage / asan would trap

struct MyType&lt;'a: 'c, 'b: 'a, 'c&gt;(&'c mut &'a str, &'b str);

impl&lt;'a: 'c, 'b: 'a, 'c&gt; Drop for MyType&lt;'a, 'b, 'c&gt; {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        // Type system thinks self.1 lives at least as long as 'a
        *self.0 = self.1;
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut me_out = "rarw";
    {
        let l = String::from("some cool string");
        let mut q = MyType(&mut me_out, "");
        q.1 = &l; // assigns a short-lived &str into a field that required 'b: 'a
    } // l dropped; Drop of q already wrote &l into me_out
    println!("me_out: {}", me_out);
}
$ rustc --version
rustc 1.99.0 (b940084d7 2026-09-28)
$ rustc uaf_covariant_field.rs -o uaf_covariant_field
$ ./uaf_covariant_field
me_out: &lt;non-deterministic garbage bytes&gt;
(Exact bytes vary; AddressSanitizer builds crash with stack-use-after-scope — see the Godbolt links on the issue.)


Case 2: 'static bound bypass with a kept value
The original report uses a Mutex&lt;Vec&lt;Box&lt;dyn Any + Send&gt;&gt;&gt; to keep a T: 'static value that was actually a short reference. On 1.99.0 here it compiled and printed the later stack string that reused the slot:
// uaf_kept_static.rs — original-report shape; rustc 1.99.0 prints the filler String
use std::sync::Mutex;

static KEPT: Mutex&lt;Vec&lt;Box&lt;dyn std::any::Any + Send&gt;&gt;&gt; = Mutex::new(Vec::new());

struct Q&lt;T: 'static + Send&gt;(Option&lt;T&gt;);

impl&lt;T: 'static + Send&gt; Drop for Q&lt;T&gt; {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        if let Some(t) = self.0.take() {
            KEPT.lock().unwrap().push(Box::new(t));
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    {
        let l = String::from("hello world, this is long enough");
        let mut q = Q(None);
        q.0 = Some(&l); // T inferred as &'static String, but &l is not 'static
    }
    let _z = String::from("XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX");
    let k = KEPT.lock().unwrap();
    println!("{}", k[0].downcast_ref::&lt;&'static String&gt;().unwrap());
}
$ rustc uaf_kept_static.rs -o uaf_kept_static && ./uaf_kept_static
XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
That output is the smoking gun: the kept &String no longer points at "hello world…".


Case 3: What to check in your code
You are looking for custom Drop (or drop glue that runs user code) combined with post-init assignment into lifetime-carrying fields, especially where the type constructor / bounds promised 'static or a long lifetime. Ordinary &T fields without clever Drop are the common case and are not automatically vulnerable. Do not “fix” this by sprinkling unsafe — wait for a compiler fix; track #164250.
Miri (when available on nightly) reports UB on related reproducers (dangling reference in a spawned closure). This box did not run Miri; the stable rustc runs above are enough to show acceptance + observable UAF.

The whole program
// Life before main without crates: a function pointer placed in .init_array.
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU32, Ordering};

static INIT_CALLS: AtomicU32 = AtomicU32::new(0);

extern "C" fn early_init() {
    INIT_CALLS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
    // Only use things that need no runtime setup: raw write(2) to stdout.
    let msg = b"[early_init] running before main\n";
    unsafe { libc_write(1, msg.as_ptr().cast(), msg.len()) };
}

unsafe extern "C" {
    #[link_name = "write"]
    fn libc_write(fd: i32, buf: *const core::ffi::c_void, n: usize) -&gt; isize;
}

#[used]
#[unsafe(link_section = ".init_array")]
static EARLY_INIT: extern "C" fn() = early_init;

// A lazily-initialised alternative that needs no linker tricks.
static CONFIG: std::sync::LazyLock&lt;String&gt; = std::sync::LazyLock::new(|| {
    println!("[LazyLock] initialising on first use");
    "mode=fast".to_string()
});

fn main() {
    println!("main: INIT_CALLS = {}", INIT_CALLS.load(Ordering::Relaxed));
    println!("main: before touching CONFIG");
    println!("main: CONFIG = {}", *CONFIG);
    println!("main: CONFIG again = {}", *CONFIG);
}
Notes on the choices:

early_init is extern "C" because the loader calls it with the platform C ABI, not Rust’s ABI.
It calls only write(2). The ctor docs give the rule plainly: constructor code “should be careful to limit itself to libc functions and code that does not rely on Rust’s stdlib services.” println! takes the stdout lock and touches a lazily set-up Stdout. That happens to work on glibc today, but nothing promises it, so the constructor avoids it.
The write declaration uses *const core::ffi::c_void. My first draft used *const u8, and rustc warned that this was a “suspicious definition of the runtime write symbol”, because it clashes with std’s own declaration of the same symbol. Match the C prototype exactly.

Unit Tests, Integration Tests, and Doctests
Rust has first-class built-in testing support.

Unit Tests
Unit tests live in the same source files as the code they test inside a #[cfg(test)] module:
pub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -&gt; i32 {
    a + b
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_add() {
        assert_eq!(add(2, 3), 5);
    }
}


Integration Tests
Integration tests live in the top-level tests/ directory and test the public API as an external consumer.


Documentation Tests (Doctests)
Examples written in markdown doc-comments (///) are compiled and executed during cargo test.

Writing Great Documentation
Write documentation comments using triple slashes /// supporting Markdown:
/// Calculates the checksum for a given network payload buffer.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// let data = [1, 2, 3];
/// let sum: u32 = data.iter().map(|&x| x as u32).sum();
/// assert_eq!(sum, 6);
/// ```
pub fn calculate_checksum(data: &[u8]) -&gt; u32 {
    data.iter().map(|&x| x as u32).sum()
}
Generate and open documentation:
cargo doc --open

A harmless look-alike
proc-macro-two is a local path crate. Its build.rs does what a malicious one would do first, but only reports it: it counts environment variables (where CI tokens live), counts entries in ~/.ssh, tries an outbound TCP connection, and writes a file outside target/.
#!/bin/sh
# An app that depends on a look-alike crate. Its build.rs is harmless, but it
# shows what any build script can do: read your environment and files, write
# outside target/, and open network connections, all before main() exists.
set -e
rm -rf app proc-macro-two /tmp/buildrs-was-here
cargo new -q --lib proc-macro-two
cat &gt; proc-macro-two/build.rs &lt;&lt;'RS'
use std::{env, fs, net::TcpStream, time::Duration};
fn main() {
    let home = env::var("HOME").unwrap_or_default();
    let ssh = fs::read_dir(format!("{home}/.ssh")).map(|d| d.count()).unwrap_or(0);
    let vars = env::vars().count();
    let net = match "1.1.1.1:443".parse() {
        Ok(a) =&gt; match TcpStream::connect_timeout(&a, Duration::from_secs(2)) {
            Ok(_) =&gt; "network: connected to 1.1.1.1:443".to_string(),
            Err(e) =&gt; format!("network: {e}"),
        },
        Err(_) =&gt; unreachable!(),
    };
    let report = format!("build.rs ran as uid {}: saw {vars} env vars, {ssh} entries in ~/.ssh; {net}\n",
        fs::read_to_string("/proc/self/status").unwrap_or_default()
            .lines().find(|l| l.starts_with("Uid:")).unwrap_or("").split_whitespace().nth(1).unwrap_or("?"));
    fs::write("/tmp/buildrs-was-here", &report).unwrap();
    println!("cargo::warning={}", report.trim());
}
RS
cargo new -q app
cd app
cargo add -q --path ../proc-macro-two

